---
<CaseLayout
  title="BookFast Checkout Flow"
  kicker="04 · B2B · Web · 2021"
  tone="green"
  description="Lead designer for simplifying BookFast’s back-office checkout, so merchants can check customers out faster."
  lead="Checkout time directly shapes a merchant’s service. The goal was to help merchants check customers out quickly and complete more sales, so customers don’t give up on a purchase because of a long checkout queue."
  meta={[
    { label: 'Timeline', value: 'Dec 2021 – Jan 2022' },
    { label: 'Team', value: '2 people (excluding engineering)' },
    { label: 'Platform', value: 'Web' },
  ]}
  toc={toc}
  next={{ href: lurl('en', '/cases/myvideo/'), title: 'myVideo Member Center Redesign' }}
>
  <Fragment slot="hero"><BS kind="hero" /></Fragment>

  <h2 id="why">Why simplify checkout?</h2>
  <p>An accurate, fast checkout helps merchants serve customers faster. Speed matters, but making sure nothing goes wrong during checkout was the bigger design challenge. The main goal was to work out which information is truly needed and streamline the flow.</p>

  <h3>Goal</h3>
  <p>Checkout time directly shapes a merchant’s service: when checkout is slow, customers come away with a worse impression of the business. So the main goal was <span class="hl">helping merchants check out faster and complete more sales, so customers don’t give up on a purchase because of a long checkout queue</span>.</p>

  <h3>Challenges</h3>
  <p>For a B2B product, a simple checkout flow makes it easier to serve different types of businesses, but it also has to meet each one’s needs and match how they think about checkout.</p>

  <h3>My role</h3>
  <p>Planned and led the UX research, the back-office logic, the testing approach and the UI.</p>
  <p class="muted">The redesign launched in Taiwan on January 17, 2022.</p>

  <hr />

  <h2 id="goals">Goals</h2>
  <p>Before starting, we set clear goals to keep the work focused and to give us good criteria for research and testing:</p>
  <BF kind="goals" />

  <h3>Problems with the old flow</h3>
  <BS kind="old" />
  <p>In the old back-office checkout, the payment amount couldn’t be changed once staff reached the invoice step, and the screens showed a lot of loosely organized information.</p>

  <hr />

  <h2 id="research">Research</h2>
  <BF kind="method" />

  <h3>Desk research</h3>
  <p>We studied common checkout flows and how different industries handle checkout, to set the direction for the redesign.</p>
  <BF kind="compare" />

  <hr />

  <h2 id="iterate">Design and test iterations</h2>
  <p>With a short timeline for design and development, we used cognitive walkthroughs to test the design. To avoid skewed results from repeat testing, each round used different participants.</p>
  <div class="walk">
    <p class="walk-t">Cognitive walkthrough setup</p>
    <dl>
      <div><dt>Venue</dt><dd>A mid-sized gym that issues invoices on site and supports mobile invoice carriers. Accepts cash and credit cards.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Persona</dt><dd>Front-desk staff who help members check out.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Scenario</dt><dd>It’s the end of the month, and many members are queuing to buy next month’s membership. The first member wants to pay by credit card and save the invoice to their mobile carrier.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Task</dt><dd>Help the member check out, making sure everything is correct along the way.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Participants</dt><dd>Colleagues, mainly from customer service, sales and design</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Tool</dt><dd>Figma</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </div>

  <h3>Version 1.0</h3>
  <p>This version showed more information and kept checkout in separate steps.</p>
  <BS kind="v1" />
  <div class="fb">
    <p class="fb-t">Feedback</p>
    <p>After choosing the invoice type, there was nothing to review with the customer before finishing; you had to go back a step, or checkout would just complete. If something was wrong, you’d have to void the sale and start again.</p>
    <p>The carrier options were long and took a while to read; you’d only remember where things are once you’re very familiar with it.</p>
    <p>The final confirmation step didn’t feel very useful.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Version 2.0</h3>
  <BS kind="v2" />
  <div class="fb">
    <p class="fb-t">Feedback</p>
    <p>It was clear what information was there, and the flow could be completed. But with many customers waiting, it still didn’t feel faster; there was simply too much to check.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Version 3.0 (final)</h3>
  <p>The step-by-step choices became a single page, and the summary column on the left updates live with each setting, so staff can confirm the details as they go and be sure everything is correct at checkout.</p>
  <BS kind="v3" />
  <div class="fb final">
    <p class="fb-t">Feedback</p>
    <p>Across three rounds of testing, this version had the fastest completion time and the highest success rate, so it became the final version.</p>
  </div>
</CaseLayout>

